<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<h4>The Big Question: the Ethiopian official</h4>
<p>The story of the Ethiopian eunuch (Acts 8:26-39) is perfect for opening the session. Do not read it as an ancient text; tell it as a living story. The Ethiopian was a high official, cultured, sincerely seeking God. He was reading the prophet Isaiah without understanding. Philip runs alongside his chariot. It is an image of evangelization: the Church drawing near to the seeker. The water was there, by the roadside. It was not a mighty river or a sacred pool: it was a puddle in the desert. God's grace does not need perfect conditions.</p>

<h4>Plunging, not sprinkling</h4>
<p>The word &laquo;Baptism&raquo; means to immerse or plunge. It is not a superficial ritual. Explain that in the early Church, Baptism was by full immersion: the catechumen descended into the pool, was plunged three times, and emerged. This expresses perfectly the death and resurrection with Christ (Rom 6:3-4). Pouring (infusion) is also valid and more common today, but students should know the original meaning.</p>

<h4>The indelible character</h4>
<p>This point often makes an impact. Explain that Baptism imprints a seal that can never be erased. Neither sin, nor apostasy, nor abandonment of the faith can remove that mark. This is a great consolation: God does not regret having made us his own. But it is also a responsibility: we bear Christ's mark forever; our life must correspond to it.</p>

<h4>Original sin</h4>
<p>Many people misunderstand original sin. It is not a personal sin committed by Adam that is imputed to us. It is a state: we are born deprived of sanctifying grace, with a wounded nature, inclined to evil. Baptism heals that wound and restores divine life. Explain this positively: Baptism does not merely cleanse; it elevates; it makes us children of God, not simply &laquo;non-sinners.&raquo;</p>

<h4>Infant Baptism</h4>
<p>The question &laquo;Why baptize babies if they cannot decide?&raquo; will almost certainly arise. The answer is that grace is a gift, not a conquest. Parents give their children what they need without waiting for their consent. Faith is a gift asked for the child; the child will make it his or her own personally as they grow. Compare with circumcision in the Old Testament: Jewish babies entered the covenant without choosing it. Baptism fulfills that figure.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Age Adaptation</h3>

<h4>🌱 Seed (ages 7-12)</h4>
<p>Visuals are key. Bring images of Noah's ark, the crossing of the Red Sea, and Jesus' Baptism. Use water and a seashell as props. Have them draw their own Baptism (even if they do not remember it). Teach them to make the sign of the cross with water and to say: &laquo;I am a child of God.&raquo; Memorize Jn 3:5 with gestures. The idea of &laquo;new birth&raquo; clicks with the image of a baby being born.</p>

<h4>🌿 Sprouts (ages 13-17)</h4>
<p>Adolescence is a search for identity: &laquo;Who am I?&raquo; Baptism answers: you are a child of God, marked forever, loved unconditionally. If they know with certainty who they are, they do not need to search in the wrong places. Connect with the Confirmation many of them are preparing for: Baptism is the root; Confirmation is the ripe fruit. Ask whether their godparents fulfill their role, or whether they themselves could be good godparents one day.</p>

<h4>🪴 Root (adults new to the faith)</h4>
<p>Many adults approaching the faith come from traditions where baptism was merely a symbol. Help them discover that it is a real transformation with objective effects. Some will be in the catechumenate or will have recently completed it: invite them to share their experience. There may also be adults baptized as infants who never made the faith their own; this topic can be an awakening for them.</p>

<h4>🌳 Tree (formed adults)</h4>
<p>Go deeper into sacramental theology: the relationship between Baptism and the Paschal Mystery (Rom 6), the Trinitarian dimension of the sacrament, ecclesial incorporation. You can quote the Fathers: St. Cyril of Jerusalem, whose <em>Mystagogical Catecheses</em> are a gem on Baptism; St. Thomas Aquinas on the sacramental character (<em>Summa Theologiae</em> III, q.63); and the documents of the Second Vatican Council on the common priesthood of the faithful (<em>Lumen Gentium</em> 10-12).</p>

</section>

