<section class="content">

<h3>Difficult Questions</h3>

<h4>&laquo;Why does the Church baptize babies? They cannot choose!&raquo;</h4>
<p>Because Baptism is a gift of grace, not a reward for personal decision. No one asks a baby whether it wants to be born, fed, or vaccinated: it is given what it needs to live. Faith is supernatural life; parents ask for it on behalf of their children. The child will grow and make that faith his or her own personally at Confirmation and in every decision of their Christian life. The Church has baptized infants since apostolic times (Acts 16:15,33; 1 Cor 1:16) (CCC 1250-1252).</p>

<h4>&laquo;What about those who have never heard of Christ? Are they all damned?&raquo;</h4>
<p>The Church teaches that Baptism is necessary for salvation for those who have received the Gospel (CCC 1257). However, God is not bound by the sacraments. Those who, through no fault of their own, do not know Christ or his Church, but sincerely seek God and strive to do his will, can attain salvation. This has been called &laquo;Baptism of desire&raquo; (CCC 1260). There is also &laquo;Baptism of blood&raquo;: whoever dies for the faith before receiving the sacrament is baptized by their martyrdom (CCC 1258). The Church trusts in God's mercy while remaining faithful to Christ's command to baptize.</p>

<h4>&laquo;I was baptized as a baby but have never practiced the faith. Do I need to be baptized again?&raquo;</h4>
<p>No. Baptism imprints an indelible character. Once you are baptized, you belong to Christ forever. Baptism cannot be repeated. If you have been away, the path back is through Confession, which restores the baptismal grace lost through grave sin. You can renew your baptismal promises, especially at the Easter Vigil. But you do not need a second Baptism (CCC 1272).</p>

<h4>&laquo;What about children who die without Baptism? That seems so unfair.&raquo;</h4>
<p>This question touches the heart. The Church entrusts them to the mercy of God. Jesus said: &laquo;Let the children come to me, do not hinder them, for to such belongs the kingdom of God&raquo; (Mk 10:14). There are reasons for hope. The Church does not teach with certainty what their destiny is, but trusts that God, who desires the salvation of all (1 Tim 2:4), acts with infinite mercy (CCC 1261).</p>

<h4>&laquo;If I already have Baptism, why do I need the other sacraments?&raquo;</h4>
<p>Baptism is the door, not the destination. It makes you a child of God, but divine life needs to grow. Confirmation strengthens that life with the gifts of the Holy Spirit. The Eucharist feeds it. Penance heals it when wounded. Anointing strengthens it in illness. Baptism gives you everything needed to begin, but the Christian journey is long, and God gives us sacraments for every stage of the way (CCC 1212).</p>

</section>

